<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adad-nirari III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 811–783 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adad-nirari III was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> from about 811 to 783 BCE, succeeding his father Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> V while young, possibly under the influence of his mother Sammuramat. His campaigns to the west weakened Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and a stele from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-al-rimah">Tell al-Rimah</a> records tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoash-of-israel">Joash of Israel</a>, whose recovery of towns from the Arameans followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-iii.jpg" alt="Adad-nirari III" width="320" height="564" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adad-nirari_III_transparent.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
